.TH CCOL_ITER_DESTROY 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_iter_destroy \- destroy a unified iterator before the end
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/citerators.h>   /* or any container header */

ccol_iter_destroy(it);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro destroys the iterator. It calls the _free_fn callback of the
iterator, and it then sets it to NULL. Use this macro to free the iterator
at the point where your own for loop breaks out. The iterator then does not
stay alive until the end of the scope.

With ccol_iter_declare(), the RAII cleanup destroys the iterator
automatically at the end of the scope. You need ccol_iter_destroy() only
when you must free the resources before the end of that scope. Inside
ccol_for_each(), the macro always destroys the iterator at the exit.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
it   Iterator variable that ccol_iter_declare() declares.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
A call is safe when it is NULL already. The macro then does nothing.
After this call, it is NULL. Do not use it again.

The macro evaluates it exactly once. It must be a modifiable lvalue,
such as a variable or an element of an array.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_iter_declare (3),
.BR ccol_iter_next (3),
.BR ccol_for_each (3)
